a this  one i would consider probably  perhaps the biggest leap in a sense this  was so  i was working at frame and we  collaborated with with tendril which is  a studio in  in um and it was uh so it was a big kind  of prestige project for a nike shoe so  that's that's kind of like one of the  you know i guess one of my dreams as a  as a young motion designer was to do  work on one of those kind of shoe  commercials um  so and and plus i got the chance to to  direct or i co-directed it with  chris barry who's the creator director  at tendril  um and it was so in many ways it was a  big step up for me it was a big step up  in terms of scale of client and also  sort of being responsible for um the  the concept and stuff like that so it  was it was a great opportunity to learn  from chris  and and um see how he did things as well  and i really learned a lot from him  uh because he you know he's been  directing projects for a lot longer than  i had  uh over with ten over at tendril so and  and just the way that they dealt with  the whole process  the workflow working with a big client  and having you know a lot of freelancers  also involved in the project  i have to ask because i know the  audience is probably wondering this  how did you get that opportunity to  direct this because  prior to this you shared some personal  uh  things that you did and then obviously  working as part of a team  i'm guessing under some uh director with  a client and then now you're at this  point in 2015  with nike your dream job and you're  co-directing this with  uh the team over at tendril and a bunch  of talented people in  house how did you get to this point  where  now you are at the helm here and getting  to direct and kind of  decide where does this go i actually  think  that i got the chance to do that because  anders who's the  the the the uh the chief creative  director of frame i think he was  um he couldn't do it for some reason i'm  not quite sure i don't  quite remember but i think it could have  been that case that maybe i wasn't the  first choice but i just got lucky  uh and you know sometimes you just have  to take those opportunities  um and but i know that if i you know if  it hadn't been that one it would have  come eventually you know it was there  was a gradual progression of  of just developing my skills and and i i  always i think from fairly early on i i  had a  desire to sort of direct because i i  think i always had a leaning towards the  conceptual side of things  um and i think going all the way back to  you know  filming and editing stuff i think that  still  was kind of informing the way i work  that i tend to think of things in terms  of  a full story and a sort of story arc and  how the shots fit together more than you  know looking at specific things and also  i think because i wasn't like uh  perhaps it helped me the fact that i  wasn't the strongest designer  because as a designer you tend to focus  on more on the visuals and the specific  shots and i think because my strength  was more in in motion and editing  that that naturally has more of a you're  more thinking about  the bigger picture and how things  connect and flow together  so i think all of those things kind of  sort of helped me along that road  um and then just being given trust you  know like i still  um are really appreciative to the i've  done a lot of work with with tendril  since and that you know  they they put a lot of faith in me and  and the guys at frame as well to sort of  they didn't once they'd given me the  thing it wasn't like oh well we'll we'll  just  sort of you know give him some scraps it  was like okay you're you know you're  you're leading this thing you can  present this to the client you know i  was terrified  it was the first time i presented like a  treatment to a client  and it was nike and i was you know but  but they the fact that they backed me up  and trusted me  um went a long way as well yeah no i  think that's huge and i think this is  actually a good place to end because  we saw all of your progression um just  starting as  an animator learning things in 2d and  then exploring some  things that you wanted to learn yourself  which is stop motion and doing a passion  project there then eventually  leveling up and starting to learn 3d but  always kept those core skills along the  way  and then starting as an internet frame  in 2009 uh  you know up until the later years you  built incremental trust along the way  through the projects that you delivered  on through the ideas that you came up  with  and eventually you got a chance you got  a chance where  okay somebody has to set out this one  who's next in line  and of course the person who you feel  like  can deliver can bring something and has  been pretty consistent at that point  you give that person a shot and that was  you at that moment so  i think those are all very important  learning lessons as people  think about their career whether they're  early on or later  you're trading up and you're trading up  based off of the trust that you build  with the  clients the studios and your peers that  you work with  and the skills that you grow and  eventually develop along the way and  it's not something that just happens  instantly your journey here that we saw  this is like a 10-year thing  at least yeah way more you know it goes  all the way you know all the things that  i  you know did in school and university  and all that stuff like  fed into it i think right so i think  it's really important  for people to think about the long game  because i know how difficult it is to  see the thing that's right in front of  you and wanting  that progress that improvement right now  but it does take time  as long as you have a good attitude  about wanting to learn more  pushing yourself and still finding the  moments of fun and passion  in the things that you love 100 yeah  yeah so  i think that's a great place to end tom  thank you so much for  sharing your your work with us so tom  if people want to find more out about  you where can they go to look you up  so yeah everything's on my website  tomcreate.com  i have all my work there i also have a  blog and i also have a  an online course and you can find links  to that on the website as well  lots of stuff cooking up as well i have  a podcast on the way and some more  youtube videos so  but if you go to my website you can also  sign up for a newsletter if you want to  uh get updated on on any new stuff  that's coming out  easy love that thank you so much tom and  uh  we'll see you next time sweet thanks so  much i hope you enjoyed today's  interview with tom crait  i'm sure it gave you some insight into  how a creative director works  and how they develop their skills over  time if you liked this interview with  tom we actually did another video with  him recently  which we'll link to in the description  below if you have any questions  leave us a comment and we'll do our best  to answer it and before i go  i want to greet all of you a happy new  year  2021 is just getting started and we're  planning all kinds of content here  on the channel if you'd like to learn  something new  or see someone specific on the channel  let us know by leaving a comment below  and your suggestions will help shape  what we do here  in the coming year thanks all for  joining us i'm matthew encina  and we'll see you in the future
 

